= Sardines

_Greco-Roman / European: classical — sardines (the small clupeoid fishes named after the island of Sardinia) are part of the Greek and Roman fish-and-fishing literature; the cured-fish preparations descend continuously from antiquity through the medieval Mediterranean to the modern canned-sardine industry. Ayurveda / Chinese / Indigenous Pacific Northwest: marginal — sardines are not species-specific in the South Asian or Chinese classical canons, though small oily fish in general have folk-dietetic standing. Modern Western: substantial — sardines are the canonical_ small low-mercury oily fish_, with strong omega-3, vitamin D, and calcium contributions. Often paired or contrasted with salmon as the small-vs.-large oily-fish comparison._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Species:*
- *_Sardina pilchardus_* (Walbaum) — European pilchard / European sardine; the canonical Mediterranean and Atlantic species; family Clupeidae.
- *_Sardinops sagax_* (Jenyns) — South American / Pacific sardine; family Clupeidae; ranges of the Pacific Rim.
- *_Sardinella_* species — tropical sardines (Atlantic, Indo-Pacific); multiple species.
- *_Sprattus sprattus_* — European sprat; small clupeoid, often canned and labeled "sardine" in commerce.
The commercial "sardine" is a *category* covering small clupeoid fishes of multiple species — typically up to ~25 cm and small enough to be canned whole.

*Latin / English:* _Sardine_ from Latin _sarda_ ("sardine, salt-cured tunny"), reportedly from Greek σαρδίνη (_sardínē_), the latter from the island name Σαρδών (_Sardṓ_, Sardinia) — where the fish was famously caught and cured. So *sardines are named after Sardinia*, the Roman center of the small-fish curing industry. (The Sardinia eponym is the standard derivation, though some scholars propose alternative routes.)#footnote[Etymology: Liddell & Scott, _A Greek–English Lexicon_ (s.v. _σαρδίνη_, _Σαρδών_); Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _sarda_); the Sardinia eponym is the standard derivation but not uncontested. _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "sardine, n.").]

*English:* _Sardine_ — entered via Old French _sardine_.

*Portuguese / Spanish:* _sardinha_ / _sardina_ — central to Iberian fish-cuisine traditions.

*Chinese:* 沙丁魚 (shādīngyú) — phonetic transliteration of "sardine"; modern Chinese; no classical entry.

*Japanese:* 鰯 (iwashi) — the generic Japanese term for small clupeoid fish including sardines, anchovies; an iconic Japanese food fish. The character 鰯 is striking: fish radical 魚 + 弱 (weak / fragile), reflecting the small and easily-bruised nature of the fish. 真鰯 (ma-iwashi) = Sardinops melanostictus, the Japanese sardine.

*Sanskrit:* No species-specific name; matsya general category.

== Classical Greco-Roman tradition

Sardines were a major component of ancient Mediterranean fishing and curing.

- *Greek and Roman commercial fishing*: sardine and tunny fisheries of the Mediterranean were major industries; Sicily, Sardinia, and southern Italy / North Africa were centers.
- *Garum and salt-curing*: the Roman _garum_ (fermented fish sauce) was made principally from small clupeoid fishes (sardines, anchovies) and tunny entrails; the most-traded sauce of the Roman empire; the Greek _gáron_ predates it. The cured-fish-sauce tradition descends continuously through Italian _colatura di alici_, Vietnamese _nước mắm_, Thai _nam pla_, and Filipino _patis_.
- *Dioscorides* and *Pliny* both mention salt-cured sardines and the related fish-sauce trade.
- *Allec* (the fish residue from garum production) — a separate Roman foodstuff.
- *Roman aristocracy* prized fresh sardines from specific Mediterranean fishing grounds.#footnote[Greco-Roman fishing literature: Curtis, _Garum and Salsamenta: Production and Commerce in Materia Medica_ (Brill, 1991), the standard work on Roman fish-curing economy; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ IX (fish); Athenaeus, _Deipnosophistae_. Garum: extensive classical and modern literature.]

== Iberian, Italian, and modern Mediterranean tradition

- *Portuguese sardines (sardinhas)*: foundational to Portuguese cuisine; *sardinhas assadas* (grilled sardines) are an iconic Portuguese summer food, especially during the *Festas de Lisboa* (June; Festa de Santo António, when grilled sardines are eaten by the millions); the smell of grilling sardines is identified with Portuguese summer identity.
- *Italian sardines (sarde)*: Sicilian _pasta con le sarde_, Venetian _sarde in saor_ (sweet-and-sour marinated), Ligurian preparations.
- *Spanish*: in Galicia and Cantabria; *sardinas en lata* (canned sardines) developed in 19th c.
- *French canned sardines*: from Nantes; the modern canning industry begins in the early 19th c. with Nicolas Appert's hermetic-sealing innovation, applied to sardines from the 1820s; *boutique aged canned sardines* are a French connoisseur tradition (millésimés, aged for years).

== Japanese tradition (iwashi)

- *Iwashi* (鰯) — sardine; one of the iconic Japanese small fishes.
- *Niboshi (煮干し)* — dried baby sardines used for dashi (stock); ubiquitous in Japanese cuisine.
- *Mezashi (目刺し)* — salt-cured small sardines on skewers; classical Japanese folk food.
- *Iwashi sashimi*: in fresh form.

== Modern Brazilian tradition

Brazilian Atlantic _sardinha verdadeira_ (_Sardinella brasiliensis_) is a foundational coastal-Brazilian food and one of the most-consumed fish in Brazil; central to Brazilian working-class fish cuisine; tied to the coastal economy of the Brazilian South-Southeast.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No species-specific classical placement; general matsya category applies. For oily small fish, the same general placement as for salmon (see salmon.typ): madhura rasa; guru-snigdha guṇa; uṣṇa vīrya; madhura vipāka; pacifies Vāta, aggravates Kapha-Pitta; balya, bṛṃhaṇa, vṛṣya. Fish-milk saṃyoga viruddha applies._

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Sardines and small Mediterranean clupeoid fish are part of the medieval Arabic *samak* category, with the salt-cured / fermented small-fish industry centered on the Mediterranean Roman *garum* tradition documented in the Arabic-language continuation *ṣīr* (صير, "salted fish-sauce"). The medieval Arabic medical literature treats small fresh fish as the lightest and most-digestible *samak* class, suitable for convalescent and digestive registers; the salt-cured forms as Hot-and-Dry and more inflammatory. See _fish.typ_ for the broader *samak* framework and _tibb-al-arabi.typ_. The Arabic *sardīn* (سردين) name is direct from Latin/Romance via Mediterranean trade.

*Reasoned Tibb placement* (no species-specific Avicennan attestation): Hot-Moist mizāj (oily fresh fish class); *muqawwī al-jasad*, *mughadhdhī*, *muḥarrik al-bāh*. The Mediterranean *salt-cured ṣīr / garum* tradition was treated by some medieval Arabic authors (al-Rāzī mentions it briefly) as warming-and-condimental, suitable for cold-constitution appetite-loss but inappropriate for inflammatory or febrile conditions.

Composition, sardines, canned in water, drained, approximate per 100 g — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~210 kcal; Water ~60 g
- Protein ~25 g (high quality)
- *Fat ~11 g*
- *Long-chain omega-3 (EPA + DHA) ~1.5–2.0 g per 100 g* — among the highest in commonly-consumed fish
- *Calcium ~380 mg per 100 g* — *exceptional* for the bones-included canned form; the canonical low-cost dietary calcium source
- Vitamin D ~5–10 µg per 100 g
- Vitamin B12 ~9 µg (very high)
- Selenium ~50 µg
- Iron ~3 mg
- Phosphorus, potassium
- *Purines* — substantial; relevant to gout-susceptible individuals

*Pharmacology of note (sardines vs. salmon — the small-vs.-large oily-fish comparison):*
- *Mercury*: sardines are *very low mercury* (small, short-lived, low trophic level — mercury bioaccumulates with trophic level and lifespan); among the safest fish for mercury exposure. *Lower than salmon* (which is also low but somewhat higher).
- *Heavy-metal contamination broadly*: sardines lower than larger predatory fish.
- *Microplastics*: small fish from the open ocean have lower microplastic accumulation than coastal or sediment-feeding fish (modest evidence).
- *Calcium*: the bones-included canned form is a *substantial calcium source*, distinctive from boneless fillet of salmon.
- *Sustainability*: many sardine fisheries are well-managed and certified (MSC); they are a relatively sustainable choice among commercially-fished species.
- *Cost*: canned sardines are among the most cost-effective sources of omega-3, vitamin D, and calcium in the global food system.
- *Purines / gout*: sardines have high purines; relevant in gout-susceptible individuals.

*Safe daily / weekly intake:*
- *General adults*: 2–3 servings (~100 g each) of oily fish per week, with sardines a strong candidate.
- *Pregnant women*: low-mercury sardines are appropriate for the 2–3 servings/week recommendation.
- *Gout patients*: limit due to purine content.
- *Children*: appropriate at smaller portions.

